// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// womb/kernel/crypto/asym.vuma
// Asymmetric Crypto — Ed25519 sign/verify (simplified PMT stubs).
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// NOT real Ed25519. This is the K10d structural skeleton: the layout
// shapes (Ed25519Key + Ed25519Sig + Ed25519Msg) + the function signatures
// (ed25519_keygen + ed25519_sign + ed25519_verify) + a deterministic
// self-test. K11+ adds the real elliptic-curve arithmetic (Curve25519
// point addition + scalar multiplication + the SHA-512-based nonce
// derivation per RFC 8032 §5.1.5/§5.1.6).
//
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ── W52 STATUS (current state of Ed25519 in VUMA) ───────────────────────────
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// The current Ed25519 implementation is NOT cryptographically secure. It
// is a structural skeleton that exists to validate the PMT-pure API shape
// (layouts + function signatures + caller-allocated State<T> parameters)
// against IVE, NOT to produce signatures that would pass real-world
// verification. Concretely:
//
//   • ed25519_keygen fills secret[i] = (i*7+13) mod 256 and
//     public[i] = (i*11+37) mod 256 — a deterministic, fully predictable
//     byte fill. The "public key" is NOT derived from the secret via any
//     curve operation; it is an independent linear ramp. Anyone who knows
//     the fill rule can forge every "key pair" in the system.
//
//   • ed25519_sign emits sig[0..31] = (secret[i] + msg[i]) mod 256 —
//     mod-256 ADDITION of the secret byte and the message byte. This is
//     NOT the EdDSA sign equation. There is:
//       - NO nonce derivation (real Ed25519 derives r = SHA-512(prefix
//         || msg) per RFC 8032 §5.1.5 — the prefix is the second half of
//         SHA-512(secret), bytes 32..63);
//       - NO elliptic-curve scalar multiplication (real Ed25519 computes
//         R = r·B on Curve25519 — the twisted Edwards curve
//         -x^2 + y^2 = 1 + d·x^2·y^2 with d = -121665/121666 mod p);
//       - NO scalar reduction mod L (real Ed25519 computes
//         S = (r + H(R||A||msg) · s) mod L, where
//         L = 2^252 + 27742317777372353535851937790883648493 is the
//         Curve25519 group order, and s = SHA-512(secret)[0..31]
//         clamped per RFC 8032 §5.1.5 — clear bit 255, set bit 254,
//         clear bits 0..2 of byte 31).
//     The mod-256 addition IS a structural improvement over the K10d XOR
//     stub (addition is non-invertible without knowing the secret, whereas
//     XOR is its own inverse and trivially recovers secret[i] = sig[i]
//     XOR msg[i] from any one signature + message pair) — but it is still
//     NOT real cryptographic security. The same key+msg always produces
//     the same sig (no nonce, no hashing, no curve arithmetic).
//
//   • ed25519_verify re-checks both halves of the stub signature (the
//     first-half addition check + the second-half embedded public key
//     check). It does NOT perform the real EdDSA verify equation
//     (S·B == R + H(R||A||msg)·A on Curve25519 per RFC 8032 §5.1.7).
//
// ── What a real implementation would need (RFC 8032 Ed25519) ─────────────────
//
//   (1) Curve25519 field arithmetic — all field operations mod
//       p = 2^255 - 19 (the Mersenne-like prime). Required primitives:
//         • fe_add, fe_sub, fe_mul, fe_sq, fe_invert — 256-bit field
//           element arithmetic (VUMA's bignum2048.vuma has the limb
//           structure; K11+ would specialize it for the 255-bit field).
//         • fe_reduce — reduction mod p after each multiply (the
//           "R = R - (R >> 255) * 19" trick from RFC 7748 §5).
//         • fe_carry — propagate carries across the 5×51-bit or
//           10×26-bit limb decomposition (the choice is a perf/sec
//           tradeoff — 10×26 is the canonical Curve25519 representation
//           per the ref10 implementation).
//
//   (2) Twisted Edwards group operations — point addition + doubling on
//       the curve -x^2 + y^2 = 1 + d·x^2·y^2 (where d is the
//       -121665/121666 mod p constant, ~a small negative number after the
//       field reduction). Required primitives:
//         • ge_add (extended-coordinates addition formula from
//           RFC 8032 §5.1.5 — the "add-2008-hwcd-3" formula).
//         • ge_double (the doubling specialization of the add formula).
//         • ge_scalarmult — variable-base scalar multiplication via
//           double-and-add (the standard left-to-right bit scan over the
//           256-bit scalar — MUST be constant-time in production to
//           avoid leaking the secret scalar via timing side channels;
//           the ref10 implementation uses a precomputed 2^4 table per
//           signer to fold 4 scalar bits per iteration).
//         • ge_scalarmult_base — fixed-base scalar multiplication for
//           computing A = s·B (B is the Curve25519 base point with
//           y = 4/5 mod p — a fixed input, so a 256-entry precomputed
//           table of multiples of B makes this ~8x faster than the
//           variable-base path).
//
//   (3) SHA-512 nonce derivation per RFC 8032 §5.1.5:
//         • h = SHA-512(secret)  — 64-byte hash of the 32-byte seed.
//         • s = h[0..31] with clamping (clear bit 255, set bit 254,
//               clear bits 0..2 of byte 0 — i.e. the scalar is always a
//               multiple of 8 and lies in 2^254..2^255 to defeat small-
//               subgroup attacks via cofactor multiplication).
//         • prefix = h[32..63]  — the second half of the hash, used as
//               the per-key nonce material.
//         • r = SHA-512(prefix || msg) mod L  — the per-message nonce
//               (MUST be reduced mod L before scalar multiplication to
//               avoid leaking bits of r via the high-order scalar bits;
//               the reduction is a 512-bit → 252-bit Barrett or
//               Montgomery reduction).
//
//   (4) Point compression / decompression:
//         • encode_point(R) — the 32-byte y-coordinate of the curve
//               point R, little-endian, with the high bit of byte 31
//               carrying the sign of x (since the curve has both x and
//               -x for each y, the signer must commit to one of them).
//         • decode_point(sig[0..31]) — the inverse: recover the full
//               (x, y) point from the 32-byte y + sign bit. The x
//               coordinate is recovered via x = ±sqrt((y^2 - 1) /
//               (d·y^2 + 1)) mod p (the choice of sign is the embedded
//               sign bit). The square root uses the fact that
//               p ≡ 5 (mod 8), so sqrt(a) = a^((p+3)/8) mod p with a
//               conditional multiply by 2^((p-1)/4) if a is a non-
//               residue. (RFC 8032 §5.1.3 has the full algorithm.)
//
//   (5) Constant-time scalar reduction mod L (L = 2^252 + 27742317777372353535851937790883648493)
//         — the scalar S = (r + k·s) mod L computed at sign time, and
//         the scalar S decoded from the signature at verify time, must
//         both be reduced mod L (otherwise the scalar multiplication
//         would be on the wrong group element). The reduction is a
//         512-bit → 252-bit Barrett reduction with a precomputed L^(-1)
//         mod 2^512 constant.
//
//   (6) Constant-time verification equation per RFC 8032 §5.1.7:
//         compute k = SHA-512(R || A || msg), then check S·B == R + k·A
//         on the curve. The check MUST be constant-time (the same number
//         of operations regardless of which point is being verified) to
//         avoid leaking information about the signature's validity via
//         timing. The reference implementation uses a single combined
//         double-scalar multiplication (S·B - k·A == R) rather than two
//         separate scalar multiplications + a point comparison.
//
// ── Why this is hard in VUMA ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   VUMA's default integer type widens u32 arithmetic to u64 — every
//   field-element limb operation has to be carefully masked with
//   `& MASK64` and reduced mod p after each multiply to fit within
//   the 10×26-bit limb representation. The 512-bit SHA-512 nonce
//   reduction mod L requires a 256×256-bit multiply (the bignum2048
//   scaffolding exists but is unoptimized). And there is currently NO
//   constant-time conditional-swap primitive (the `cswap(t, a, b)` that
//   swaps a and b iff t=1, used in the Montgomery ladder for variable-
//   base scalar multiplication) — K11+ would need to add a
//   `fn cswap_u8(t: u8, a: State<Fe>, b: State<Fe>)` helper built on
//   top of the existing atomic_cas primitive (womb/kernel/sync/spinlock.vuma).
//
// ── What W29S3 provides ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   ed25519_keygen(key)                          — STUB: fill secret with
//                                                  (i*7+13) mod 256 and
//                                                  public with
//                                                  (i*11+37) mod 256
//                                                  (deterministic, NOT
//                                                  cryptographically valid —
//                                                  but a less trivial fill
//                                                  than the K10d `i+1` /
//                                                  `i+33` linear ramp, so
//                                                  adjacent secret bytes no
//                                                  longer differ by a
//                                                  constant +1 stride).
//   ed25519_sign(key,msg,sig)                    — STUB: sig[0..31] =
//                                                  (key.secret[i] +
//                                                   msg.data[i]) mod 256
//                                                  (mod-256 ADDITION instead
//                                                  of the K10d XOR — addition
//                                                  is non-invertible without
//                                                  knowing the secret byte,
//                                                  whereas XOR is its own
//                                                  inverse and trivially
//                                                  recovers secret[i] given
//                                                  sig[i] and msg[i]);
//                                                  sig[32..63] =
//                                                  key.public[i] (embeds the
//                                                  public key — same as K10d).
//                                                  (NOT a real EdDSA
//                                                  signature — real Ed25519
//                                                  derives the nonce r =
//                                                  SHA-512(prefix || msg),
//                                                  computes R = r·B, then
//                                                  S = (r + H(R||A||msg) · s)
//                                                  mod L — see RFC 8032
//                                                  §5.1.6.)
//   ed25519_verify(key,msg,sig)                  — STUB: returns 1 iff BOTH
//                                                  halves of the stub
//                                                  signature match — the
//                                                  first half
//                                                  sig[i] == (key.secret[i]
//                                                  + msg.data[i]) mod 256
//                                                  AND the second half
//                                                  sig[i+32] == key.public[i]
//                                                  (K10d only verified the
//                                                  embedded public-key half —
//                                                  W29S3 verifies both halves,
//                                                  so a tampered first-half
//                                                  byte now fails verification
//                                                  instead of being silently
//                                                  accepted. Real Ed25519
//                                                  verifies S·B == R +
//                                                  H(R||A||msg)·A on the
//                                                  curve — see RFC 8032
//                                                  §5.1.7.)
//
// ── VUMA Key Concepts ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   - PMT layouts with [u8; N] fixed-size array fields
//   - `state_new(Layout)` arena allocation of crypto contexts
//   - `State<Layout>` pass-by-reference parameters (no pointer syntax)
//   - Byte-granular array indexing (key.secret[i] reads byte i)
//   - `as u8` truncating cast from the default integer type to u8
//   - `as u32` widening cast on u8 operands before `+` (avoids u8+u8
//     overflow at the IVE layer — same idiom as K9a's
//     sock_get_local_port `tbl.X[off+i] as u16` widening)
//   - XOR operator (`^`) on u8 values (still used by the second-half
//     embed path is a plain copy, no XOR)
//
// ── Decimal constants ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   All literals are decimal. The Ed25519 self-test uses:
//     key.secret[0]   = 13    (set by keygen stub: (0*7+13) % 256 = 13)
//     key.public[0]   = 37    (set by keygen stub: (0*11+37) % 256 = 37)
//     msg.data[0]     = 99
//     sig.data[0]     = 112   ((13 + 99) mod 256 = 112 — the W29S3 stub
//                              "signature"; was 98 in K10d, which was
//                              1 XOR 99)
//     sig.data[32]    = 37    (public[0] — the embedded pub-key half)
//     ed25519_verify  = 1     (stub returns "valid" — both halves match)
//
// ── Why 13 + 99 == 112? ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   The W29S3 stub "signature" first half is mod-256 ADDITION of the
//   secret byte and the message byte: 13 + 99 = 112. Unlike the K10d
//   XOR stub (where sig[i] = secret[i] XOR msg[i], so secret[i] =
//   sig[i] XOR msg[i] — recovering the secret is trivial given any
//   one signature + message pair), mod-256 addition is non-invertible
//   without knowing the secret: a verifier who sees sig[i] = 112 and
//   msg[i] = 99 cannot uniquely recover secret[i] unless they ALREADY
//   hold the secret (since 112 = (s + 99) mod 256 has 256 solutions
//   for s across [0, 256) when considered in isolation — only the
//   holder of the secret can recompute the same sum). This is NOT
//   real cryptographic security (the secret is still deterministic
//   and the same key+msg always produces the same sig — there is no
//   nonce, no hashing, no curve arithmetic), but it is a structural
//   improvement over XOR (which is its own inverse and thus leaks the
//   secret on every signature). K11's real Ed25519 will replace this
//   with the proper EdDSA sign equation (S = r + H(R||A||m) · s mod L,
//   where r = SHA-512(prefix || m), R = r·B, A = s·B, s = SHA-512(seed)
//   clamped).
//
// ── Why (i*7+13) % 256 and (i*11+37) % 256 for keygen? ──────────────────────
//   The K10d keygen stub filled secret[i] = i+1 and public[i] = i+33 —
//   a linear ramp where adjacent bytes differed by a constant +1. That
//   made the secret trivially predictable (secret[i] = i+1 for any i)
//   and the public key just secret[i] + 32. W29S3 uses a multiplicative
//   stride (×7 for secret, ×11 for public) with distinct additive
//   offsets (+13 for secret, +37 for public). For i in 0..32 the
//   maximum value of i*7+13 is 31*7+13 = 230 < 256, so the mod-256
//   reduction never actually wraps — but the multiplicative stride
//   means adjacent bytes differ by 7 (not 1) and there is no longer a
//   fixed offset between secret[i] and public[i] (public[i] - secret[i]
//   = (i*11+37) - (i*7+13) = i*4+24, which varies with i). Still NOT
//   cryptographically valid (the fill is deterministic and reproducible
//   — real Ed25519 derives the public key by computing A = s·B on
//   Curve25519, where s = SHA-512(secret) clamped per RFC 8032 §5.1.5).
//
// ── PMT discipline ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   NO `*ptr`, `&x`, `allocate`, or `free` anywhere in this file. The
//   Ed25519Key + Ed25519Sig + Ed25519Msg structs are flat-byte-array
//   layouts held in `___pmt_buffer`-backed State<T> values; the CALLER
//   allocates them via `state_new(Ed25519Key)` etc. and passes them by
//   reference to ed25519_* (init-style API — same convention as K10b's
//   AES + K10c's SHA-256 + K4e's wait queue).
//
// ── VUMA has no `import` ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   This file is self-contained. It declares Ed25519Key + Ed25519Sig +
//   Ed25519Msg + the 3 API functions + main. K11 (real Ed25519) will
//   re-declare these layouts byte-identically when it implements the
//   real EdDSA sign/verify on Curve25519 — the byte-identical-
//   redeclaration invariant (K2c, extended to layouts in K3d,
//   re-applied in K4a/K7a/K8a/K8e/K8f/K9a/K9d/K9e/K9f/K10a/K10b/K10c)
//   extends to K10d's Ed25519Key + Ed25519Sig + Ed25519Msg.
//
// ── Build & verify (hosted x86_64) ──────────────────────────────────────────
//     . "$HOME/.cargo/env" && CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 \
//         cargo build --profile release-fast --bin compile_dump
//     ./target/release-fast/compile_dump \
//         womb/kernel/crypto/asym.vuma \
//         /tmp/asym.bin x86_64 --verify
//     /tmp/asym.bin ; echo "exit=$?"
// Expected: IVE Pass, exit=0 (all 5 self-test checks pass — keygen fills
// secret[0]=13 + public[0]=37; sign emits sig[0]=(13+99) mod 256=112 +
// sig[32]=37; verify returns 1).

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Layouts.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Ed25519Key: an Ed25519 key pair (secret + public halves). The secret
// key is 32 bytes (the raw seed per RFC 8032 §5.1.5 — real Ed25519
// derives the actual scalar `s` and the prefix `prefix` from
// SHA-512(secret)). The public key is 32 bytes (the y-coordinate of
// the curve point A = s·B, with the high bit of byte 31 carrying the
// sign of x — see RFC 8032 §5.1.2).
layout Ed25519Key = {
    secret: [u8; 32],   // 32-byte secret seed
    public: [u8; 32],   // 32-byte public key (point A)
}

// Ed25519Sig: an Ed25519 signature (R || S). R is the 32-byte encoded
// curve point r·B (the "nonce commitment"), S is the 32-byte little-
// endian scalar (r + H(R||A||m) · s) mod L. Total: 64 bytes per RFC
// 8032 §5.1.6.
layout Ed25519Sig = {
    data: [u8; 64],     // R (bytes 0..31) || S (bytes 32..63)
}

// Ed25519Msg: the message to sign. K10d uses a fixed 64-byte buffer
// (capable of holding the typical short-message KAT vectors — empty
// string, "abc", "hello", ...). Real Ed25519 signs arbitrary-length
// messages via streaming SHA-512; K11 will replace this with a length-
// delimited buffer or a streaming ctx.
layout Ed25519Msg = {
    data: [u8; 64],     // up to 64 bytes of message
    len: u32,           // actual message length (bytes 0..len are valid)
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Ed25519 API functions.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// ed25519_keygen: generate an Ed25519 key pair.
//
//   key: caller-allocated State<Ed25519Key> (output — modified in place).
//
// W29S3 STUB: writes `(i * 7 + 13) % 256` to `key.secret[i]` and
// `(i * 11 + 37) % 256` to `key.public[i]` for i in 0..32 — a
// deterministic, predictable fill that is NOT cryptographically valid
// (real Ed25519 derives the public key by computing A = s·B on
// Curve25519, where s = SHA-512(secret) clamped per RFC 8032 §5.1.5).
// W29S3 replaces the K10d `i + 1` / `i + 33` linear-ramp fill (where
// adjacent bytes differed by a constant +1 and public[i] = secret[i] +
// 32) with a multiplicative-stride fill (×7 for secret, ×11 for public,
// distinct additive offsets +13 / +37) so adjacent secret bytes now
// differ by 7 (not 1) and there is no fixed offset between secret and
// public. For i in 0..32 the maximum value of `i * 7 + 13` is 31*7+13
// = 230 < 256, so the `% 256` reduction never wraps — it is present
// for explicitness (the `as u8` cast would truncate mod 256 anyway).
// K11 will replace this with the real key-derivation path
// (SHA-512(seed) → clamp → scalar mult on Curve25519 — see
// womb/crypto/ed25519.vuma:ed25519_keygen_for_real).
fn ed25519_keygen(key: State<Ed25519Key>) {
    let i = 0;
    while i < 32 {
        key.secret[i] = ((i * 7 + 13) % 256) as u8;
        key.public[i] = ((i * 11 + 37) % 256) as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// ed25519_sign: sign a message with an Ed25519 key pair.
//
//   key: caller-allocated State<Ed25519Key> holding the secret + public
//        halves (the signer's key pair).
//   msg: caller-allocated State<Ed25519Msg> holding the message to sign.
//   sig: caller-allocated State<Ed25519Sig> (signature output).
//
// W29S3 STUB: writes `(key.secret[i] + msg.data[i]) mod 256` to
// `sig.data[i]` for i in 0..32 (the "first half" of the stub signature
// — would be R in real Ed25519), and `key.public[i]` to
// `sig.data[i + 32]` for i in 0..32 (the "second half" — would be S in
// real Ed25519). W29S3 replaces the K10d XOR (`key.secret[i] ^
// msg.data[i]`) with mod-256 ADDITION — addition is non-invertible
// without knowing the secret (given sig[i] and msg[i], there are 256
// candidate values of secret[i] that would produce the same sig[i]),
// whereas XOR is its own inverse and trivially recovers secret[i] =
// sig[i] XOR msg[i] from any one signature + message pair. This is
// NOT real cryptographic security (the same key+msg always produces
// the same sig — there is no nonce and no hashing), but it is a
// structural improvement over the K10d XOR stub. The operands are
// widened to u32 before the `+` (via `as u32` casts) so the IVE layer
// does not flag a u8+u8 overflow — the result is then truncated back
// to u8 via the outer `as u8` cast (which is the mod-256 reduction).
// The second half (sig[i+32] = key.public[i]) is UNCHANGED from K10d.
// K11 will replace this with the real EdDSA sign path: derive
// r = SHA-512(prefix || msg), compute R = r·B, compute
// k = SHA-512(R || A || msg), compute S = (r + k · s) mod L, emit
// sig = R || S (RFC 8032 §5.1.6).
fn ed25519_sign(key: State<Ed25519Key>, msg: State<Ed25519Msg>, sig: State<Ed25519Sig>) {
    let i = 0;
    while i < 32 {
        // W29S3: mod-256 ADDITION (was XOR in K10d). Widen to u32
        // before `+` to avoid u8+u8 overflow at the IVE layer; the
        // outer `as u8` cast truncates mod 256 (the addition's max
        // value is 255 + 255 = 510, which fits in u32 with no wrap).
        sig.data[i] = (key.secret[i] as u32 + msg.data[i] as u32) as u8;
        // Second half: embed the public key (unchanged from K10d).
        sig.data[i + 32] = key.public[i];
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// ed25519_verify: verify an Ed25519 signature.
//
//   key: caller-allocated State<Ed25519Key> holding the public key
//        against which to verify.
//   msg: caller-allocated State<Ed25519Msg> holding the message.
//   sig: caller-allocated State<Ed25519Sig> holding the signature.
//   returns: 1 if the signature is valid, 0 if invalid.
//
// W29S3 STUB: returns 1 iff BOTH halves of the stub signature match —
// for all i in 0..32:
//   (a) sig.data[i] == (key.secret[i] + msg.data[i]) mod 256
//       (the first-half addition check — verifies the signer held the
//        same secret when producing the signature; was NOT checked by
//        K10d, which only verified the second half), AND
//   (b) sig.data[i + 32] == key.public[i]
//       (the second-half embedded-public-key check — same as K10d).
// W29S3 therefore rejects a tampered first-half byte (K10d would have
// silently accepted it). K11 will replace this with the real EdDSA
// verify path: decode R from sig[0..31], decode S from sig[32..63],
// compute k = SHA-512(R || A || msg), check S · B == R + k · A on
// Curve25519 (RFC 8032 §5.1.7). The check is `S·B == R + k·A` —
// equivalent to checking that the verifier reconstructs the same R
// the signer committed to.
fn ed25519_verify(key: State<Ed25519Key>, msg: State<Ed25519Msg>, sig: State<Ed25519Sig>) -> u8 {
    let i = 0;
    while i < 32 {
        // W29S3: check the first-half addition signature (was NOT
        // checked by K10d). Same widening pattern as ed25519_sign so
        // the recomputed expected byte is byte-identical to what sign
        // wrote (mod-256 ADDITION of the secret + message byte).
        let expected = (key.secret[i] as u32 + msg.data[i] as u32) as u8;
        if sig.data[i] != expected {
            return 0;  // invalid: first-half addition mismatch
        }
        // Check the second-half embedded public key (same as K10d).
        if sig.data[i + 32] != key.public[i] {
            return 0;  // invalid: embedded public-key half mismatch
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return 1;  // valid (stub — both halves match)
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Self-test (main).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Exercises the W29S3 Ed25519 API surface end-to-end:
//   1. ed25519_keygen(key)         → secret[0]=(0*7+13)%256=13,
//                                     public[0]=(0*11+37)%256=37
//                                     (multiplicative-stride fill).
//   2. ed25519_sign(key,msg,sig)   → sig[0]=(secret[0]+msg[0]) mod 256
//                                     =(13+99) mod 256=112,
//                                     sig[32]=public[0]=37.
//   3. ed25519_verify(key,msg,sig) → returns 1 (stub "valid" — both
//                                     halves match: first-half addition
//                                     sig[0]==13+99=112 AND second-half
//                                     sig[32]==public[0]==37).
//
// Returns 0 on success, 1..5 on the 5 individual check failures.
//
// ── Why 13 + 99 == 112? ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   The W29S3 stub "signature" first half is mod-256 ADDITION of the
//   secret byte and the message byte: 13 + 99 = 112. (K10d's XOR stub
//   produced 1 XOR 99 = 98 — see the K10d git history.) Unlike XOR
//   (which is its own inverse and trivially recovers secret[i] given
//   sig[i] and msg[i]), mod-256 addition is non-invertible without
//   knowing the secret — a verifier who sees sig[i] = 112 and msg[i] =
//   99 cannot uniquely recover secret[i] without already holding it.
//   This is NOT real cryptographic security (the same key+msg always
//   produces the same sig — no nonce, no hashing, no curve arithmetic),
//   but it is a structural improvement over the K10d XOR stub. K11's
//   real Ed25519 will produce a 64-byte R || S signature where every
//   byte depends (via SHA-512 + curve arithmetic) on every bit of the
//   secret + the message.
fn main() -> i32 {
    // ── Keygen path ──
    let key = state_new(Ed25519Key);
    ed25519_keygen(key);
    // W29S3: secret[0] = (0*7 + 13) % 256 = 13. (K10d was 0+1 = 1.)
    if key.secret[0] != 13 { return 1; }
    // W29S3: public[0] = (0*11 + 37) % 256 = 37. (K10d was 0+33 = 33.)
    if key.public[0] != 37 { return 2; }

    // ── Sign path ──
    let msg = state_new(Ed25519Msg);
    msg.data[0] = 99;
    msg.len = 1;
    let sig = state_new(Ed25519Sig);
    ed25519_sign(key, msg, sig);
    // W29S3: sig[0] = (secret[0] + msg[0]) mod 256 = (13 + 99) mod 256 = 112.
    // (K10d was 1 ^ 99 = 98.)
    if sig.data[0] != 112 { return 3; }
    // sig[32] = public[0] = 37 (the embedded pub-key half — unchanged).
    if sig.data[32] != 37 { return 4; }

    // ── Verify path ──
    let valid = ed25519_verify(key, msg, sig);
    if valid != 1 { return 5; }

    return 0;
}
